---
const pains = [
  {
    label: 'Week 1',
    text: 'Auth flow. JWT refresh. Password reset. Email verification. 2FA. Google OAuth.'
  },
  {
    label: 'Week 2',
    text: 'Tenant context. Roles. Member invitations. Permission system with wildcards.'
  },
  {
    label: 'Week 3',
    text: 'Stripe. Plans. Addons. Webhooks. Idempotency. Trials. Past-due grace period.'
  },
  {
    label: 'Week 4',
    text: 'i18n. Audit logs. Rate limits. Error pages. Tests. CI. The list never ends.'
  }
]
---

<section class="border-b border-border/60">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-20 md:py-28">
    <div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-16">
      <div class="lg:col-span-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
          <span class="lk-section-num">01 / Problem</span>
        </div>
        <h2 class="lk-display lk-balance text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl">
          You've built this <em class="text-muted-foreground">exact thing</em> four times already.
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-md text-lg text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
          Every new SaaS starts the same way: three weeks of plumbing before
          you write a single line of feature code. Auth. Tenants. Stripe.
          Roles. The boring infrastructure that customers won't pay you for —
          but won't forgive you for getting wrong.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="lg:col-span-7 grid gap-3 self-end">
        {
          pains.map((pain) => (
            <li class="grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] items-baseline gap-6 border-t border-border py-5 first:border-t-0 first:pt-0">
              <span class="lk-section-num shrink-0">{pain.label}</span>
              <p class="font-display text-2xl leading-snug md:text-3xl lk-balance">
                {pain.text}
              </p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
